/-!
# The Hilbert polynomial of a coherent sheaf on projective space is a numerical polynomial

For a field `k`, an integer `n ≥ 0` and a coherent sheaf `ℱ` on `ℙⁿ_k`, the function
`d ↦ χ(ℙⁿ_k, ℱ(d))` is a polynomial. This is [Stacks, Tag 08AC], and "is a polynomial" is the
source's own notion of a *numerical polynomial*: by [Stacks, Tag 00JX] a function `f : ℤ → A`
into an abelian group is a numerical polynomial when `f d = ∑_{i ≤ r} (d choose i) • aᵢ` for all
sufficiently large `d`. That is the notion the source's proof produces, since the proof concludes
with [Stacks, Tag 00JZ], whose hypothesis and conclusion are both "is a numerical polynomial" and
whose statement is about functions defined for all sufficiently large arguments. Here it is
`IsNumericalPolynomial`.

The source's proof is an induction on `n`. For `n = 0` projective space is `Spec k`, the twists
are trivial and the function is constant. For `n > 0` a hyperplane `i : H ⟶ ℙⁿ_k` gives short
exact sequences `0 ⟶ ℱ(d-1) ⟶ ℱ(d) ⟶ i_*𝒢(d) ⟶ 0`, and additivity of the Euler characteristic
turns the first difference of `d ↦ χ(ℙⁿ_k, ℱ(d))` into `d ↦ χ(H, 𝒢(d))`, which the induction
hypothesis handles because `H ≅ ℙⁿ⁻¹_k`; [Stacks, Tag 00JZ] then returns the function itself.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.projectiveTwistZeroIso`: **the twists on `ℙ⁰` are trivial.** For any
  commutative ring `R`, any module `𝒢` on `ℙ⁰_R` and any `d ∈ ℤ`, `𝒢(d) ≅ 𝒢`.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.hilbertPolynomial_eq_eulerCharacteristic`: the Hilbert polynomial of
  [Stacks, Tag 08AD] at a twist degree is the Euler characteristic of that twist.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.hilbertPolynomial_sub_eq_eulerCharacteristic_of_shortExact`: **the first
  difference along a hyperplane sequence.** A short exact sequence of coherent modules on `ℙⁿ_k`
  whose first two terms are `ℱ(d-1)` and `ℱ(d)` has
  `χ(ℱ(d)) - χ(ℱ(d-1)) = χ` of its third term.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.hilbertPolynomial_sub_eq_eulerCharacteristic_of_shortExact'`: the same
  with the finite-dimensionality input discharged by [Stacks, Tag 02O6] over a field base, and
  so with no finiteness hypothesis at all.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.isNumericalPolynomial_hilbertPolynomial`: **[Stacks, Tag 08AC]** granted the
  hyperplane difference formula of its inductive step, the induction on `n` and its base case
  being carried out in full.

## The twists on `ℙ⁰` are trivial

On `ℙ⁰_k = Spec k` the source says "`ℱ(d) = ℱ`", and that identification is proved here, for an
arbitrary base ring and in every integer degree, as `AlgebraicGeometry.projectiveTwistZeroIso`.
`AlgebraicGeometry.projectiveTwist` is the tensor product with the inverse image of the twisting
sheaf of integral projective space, so the work is to trivialise that twisting sheaf on
`Proj ℤ[T₀]`; the route is the following chain, whose pieces are the other declarations of this
file.

* `AlgebraicGeometry.isIso_unitHomEquiv_symm_of_nonvanishingOpen_eq_top`: a global section of an
  invertible module that vanishes **nowhere** trivialises it on all of the space. This is the
  trivialization clause of [Stacks, Tag 01CY] with the nonvanishing locus taken to be everything,
  so that the conclusion is an isomorphism of modules on the space itself rather than on the
  localized site over the locus.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.unitModuleIsoTwistingSheafOneOneVariable`: on `Proj A[T₀]` — a polynomial
  ring in a **single** variable — the section `T₀/1` of `𝒪(1)` vanishes nowhere, since its
  nonvanishing locus contains `D₊(T₀)` and the irrelevant ideal of `A[T₀]` is generated by `T₀`,
  so `D₊(T₀)` is everything. Hence `𝒪 ≅ 𝒪(1)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.twistingSheafIsoUnitModuleOneVariable`: therefore `𝒪(d) ≅ 𝒪` on `Proj A[T₀]`
  for every `d ∈ ℤ`, because `𝒪(1)^{⊗ d} ≅ 𝒪(d)` ([Stacks, Tag 01CU], a polynomial ring being
  generated in degree one) and every integer tensor power of a module isomorphic to `𝒪` is
  isomorphic to `𝒪`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.projectiveTwistingSheafZeroIsoUnit`: pulling that back along the projection
  `ℙ⁰_S ⟶ ℙ⁰_ℤ` gives `𝒪(d) ≅ 𝒪` on `ℙ⁰_S` for an arbitrary base scheme `S`, invertible modules
  and the structure sheaf both being stable under inverse image.
* Tensoring with it and cancelling the structure sheaf is `projectiveTwistZeroIso`.

## What `hstep` is

It is the displayed equation of the source's inductive step, together with the identification
`H ≅ ℙⁿ⁻¹_k` under which the right-hand side becomes a Hilbert polynomial on `ℙⁿ⁻¹_k`. Its own
inputs are the short exact sequence of [Stacks, Tag 08A0], the twisted sequence and its
cohomology of [Stacks, Tag 0EGK], the identification of the hyperplane with `ℙⁿ⁻¹_k` of
[Stacks, Tag 089Z], and additivity of the Euler characteristic, [Stacks, Tag 08AA]. The last
two of those four are reduced to the sequence itself by
`AlgebraicGeometry.hilbertPolynomial_sub_eq_eulerCharacteristic_of_shortExact'`, so what `hstep`
still asks for beyond a short exact sequence is the geometry of the hyperplane.

**The base field is fixed throughout**, so [Stacks, Tag 08AB] — the source's reduction to an
infinite base field, used only to produce the hyperplane — is not needed here: `hstep` is asked
for the given field directly. It *is* needed to discharge `hstep`, since the hyperplane exists
only over an infinite field; that is done downstream.

**`hstep` is discharged**, and [Stacks, Tag 08AC] therefore proved outright, in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Variety/EulerChar/HilbertPolynomialPolynomial.lean`:
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_coherent_hilbertPolynomial_sub_eq` is `hstep` over an infinite field,
from the twisted hyperplane restriction sequences of
`AlgebraicGeometry.exists_restrictionSequences`, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.isNumericalPolynomial_hilbertPolynomial'` is the tag with no hypothesis at
all, over an arbitrary field. So nothing below is open; what `hstep` does is keep the induction
separate from the geometry that feeds it.

## Implementation notes

**[Stacks, Tag 08AC] is attached, clause-qualified**, to
`AlgebraicGeometry.isNumericalPolynomial_hilbertPolynomial`, whose only hypothesis beyond the
source's own is `hstep`. The qualifier records it. What the theorem itself supplies is the whole
of the source's argument that is not that one input: the passage from the Hilbert polynomial to
the Euler characteristic, the base case in full, the induction on `n`, and the application of
[Stacks, Tag 00JZ] through `main_00JZ`. The tag is attached a second time, with no qualifier, to
`AlgebraicGeometry.isNumericalPolynomial_hilbertPolynomial'`, which has no hypothesis.

**Coherence of the twists** is `AlgebraicGeometry.isCoherent_projectiveTwist`, made available to
instance search locally rather than globally so that no instance is added to the library.

**The grading of a polynomial ring** is supplied as a local instance in the section proving the
triviality of the twists on `ℙ⁰`, since the twisting sheaves of `Proj` are indexed by it.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 08AC](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08AC)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08AD](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08AD)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00JX](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00JX)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00JZ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00JZ)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01CU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01CU)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01CY](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01CY)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08AA](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08AA)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08A0](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08A0)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 089Z](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/089Z)
